body:
Thanks for tracking this down alan-agius! I can confirm that adding 

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    ...
    "paths": {
      "@service-work/is-loading": ["projects/is-loading/build"]
    }
  }
}
```

Does fix the build problem. I'm surprised that it is needed though. Already, because this is a yarn workspace, both `@service-work/is-loading` and `@service-work/scroll-position` are symlinked in the `node_modules` folder (though the symlinks point to the raw source rather than the "build" output). It's for this reason that the tests run without error when the `scroll-position` library imports the `is-loading` library. Why is the "build" version of `is-loading` needed in order to build `scroll-position`? Is this documented somewhere? Since `is-loading` is a peer dependency, it's not being included in `scroll-position` anyway and so, I expected, it was only used for typings during the build process. 

_As an aside, it's strange but I never got a notification that a comment was added to this issue (I've looked back through all my recently "done" notifications and confirmed that nothing exists for this issue). I wonder if it's related to the fact that the issue was moved from one repo to another._

@IanTheG It's a little unclear because you haven't included a reproduction, but it sounds like you are providing another example of a (different) error message being suppressed by the compiler-cli. It sounds like this is also incorrectly resulting in a `FatalDiagnosticError` as described by alan-agius above.

> I'm not sure why Angular would generate the public-api.ts file inside a folder when using workspaces. I'd hoped to have it work out the box, but it cost me an hour or two of problem solving.

Personally, I find this comment to be confusing. You seem to be implying that, because a file named `public-api.ts` is inside a folder your build was failing. But, in theory, so long as the relevant configuration files are setup correctly (i.e. `angular.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `ng-package.json`, etc), a library's entry file named `public-api.ts` (or something else, because it can be named anything) should have no problem being located inside a folder. If you look at my [Angular IsLoadingService repo](https://gitlab.com/service-work/is-loading) (which, thanks to alen-arguis' help, is now building properly), you'll see that the entry points for the libraries are located within folders. If you're saying that you generated a new angular library using the CLI, and that new library was not generated with the proper configuration, that sounds like a separate issue.

@alan-agius4, is there a way to get a dump of FatalDiagnosticError object, like you show?
I have the same error, probably hiding some other error, and I would like to fix the problem without waiting for the next release.
Thank you.

